# Graph Algorithms: Navigating Networks

> A graph is just dots joined by lines — enough to model roads,
> friendships, the internet, and build systems. This lesson covers
> storing graphs, traversing them, and finding shortest paths.

## 1. Graph Vocabulary

[definition]
A **graph** G = (V, E) is a set of **vertices** (nodes) and a set of
**edges** joining pairs of them. The **degree** of a vertex counts the
edges that touch it.
[/definition]

| Term | Meaning | Everyday example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Directed | edges have a direction | a Twitter follow |
| Undirected | edges go both ways | a Facebook friendship |
| Weighted | edges carry a cost | road distance |
| Path | sequence of connected edges | route home |
| Cycle | path that returns to its start | a round trip |
| DAG | directed graph with no cycles | course prerequisites |

## 2. Storing a Graph: Matrix vs List

| Representation | Space | Edge test "u to v?" | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Adjacency matrix** (V × V grid) | O(V²) | O(1) | dense graphs |
| **Adjacency list** (per-vertex list) | O(V + E) | O(degree of u) | sparse graphs |

Real networks are sparse — a person knows hundreds out of billions of
people — so adjacency lists are the default choice.

## 3. Traversals: BFS and DFS

Start from A in this graph (edges A–B, A–C, B–D, C–D, C–E):

@diagram tree id="bfs-levels" layout="tree" aria="Graph arranged by breadth-first search levels rooted at node A"
  A -> B
  A -> C
  B -> D
  C -> D
  C -> E
@/diagram

**Breadth-first search (BFS)** uses a **queue**: visit the start, then
its neighbours, then *their* neighbours — level by level. From A the
order is A, B, C, D, E.

**Depth-first search (DFS)** uses a **stack**, often the call stack:
dive down one branch to the end, backtrack, dive again. From A, trying
neighbours alphabetically: A, B, D, C, E.

| Property | BFS | DFS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Data structure | queue (FIFO) | stack (LIFO) |
| Exploration | level by level, closest first | one branch fully, then backtrack |
| Delivers | shortest paths (unweighted) | cycle detection, topological order |
| Memory | can be very wide | O(depth) — usually small |

BFS finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs because it reaches every
vertex in fewest-edges-first order. Weighted graphs need Dijkstra.

## 4. Shortest Paths: Dijkstra and Bellman-Ford

**Dijkstra's algorithm** is greedy: repeatedly settle the unsettled
vertex with the smallest tentative distance, then **relax** its edges
— if routing through it improves a neighbour's distance, update it.
On edges A–B = 4, A–C = 1, C–B = 2, C–D = 5, B–D = 1:

| Step | Settled | Remaining distances |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | A = 0 | B = 4, C = 1, D = ∞ |
| 2 | C = 1 | B = 3 via A–C–B, D = 6 |
| 3 | B = 3 | D = 4 via A–C–B–D |
| 4 | D = 4 | all settled |

With a binary heap, Dijkstra runs in O((V + E) log V). It requires
**non-negative weights**: a negative edge can make an already-settled
vertex improvable, which breaks the greedy assumption.

[important]
**Bellman-Ford** allows negative edges and detects negative cycles in
O(V·E) time — slower, but it is the tool when weights can go below
zero.
[/important]

## 5. Minimum Spanning Trees

A **spanning tree** connects all vertices with V − 1 edges and no
cycles; a **minimum spanning tree (MST)** is the cheapest such set.

| Algorithm | Strategy | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Prim | grow one tree from a start vertex, always adding the cheapest edge leaving the tree | O(E log V) |
| Kruskal | sort all edges, add each cheapest edge that does not close a cycle | O(E log E) |

Kruskal needs **union-find** to test for cycles fast; Prim needs a
priority queue. Classic uses: cheapest cabling or pipe networks, and
clustering.

## 6. Topological Sort

A **topological order** of a **DAG** lists vertices so every edge
points forward — prerequisites always come before dependents. Courses,
build steps, and package installs all need one.

@diagram tree id="course-prerequisites" layout="tree" aria="Course prerequisite chain where each course points to a course that depends on it"
  "Data Structures" -> "Algorithms"
  "Algorithms" -> "Machine Learning"
  "Linear Algebra" -> "Machine Learning"
@/diagram

A valid order here is Data Structures, Linear Algebra, Algorithms,
Machine Learning. Kahn's algorithm builds such orders by repeatedly
removing any vertex whose prerequisites are all done.

## 7. Graphs in the Real World

| Domain | Vertices / edges | Algorithm at work |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GPS navigation | intersections / roads | Dijkstra, A* |
| Internet routing | routers / links | OSPF runs Dijkstra inside one AS |
| Social networks | people / friendships | BFS gives degrees of separation |
| Build systems | files / dependencies | topological sort |
| Recommendations | users and items / links | graph similarity |

[summary]
Store sparse graphs as adjacency lists. BFS (queue) explores level by
level and finds unweighted shortest paths; DFS (stack) dives deep and
yields cycles and topological orders. Dijkstra solves non-negative
shortest paths in O((V+E) log V); Bellman-Ford copes with negative
weights; Prim and Kruskal build minimum spanning trees.
[/summary]
